<!--
  AP session-rx buffer-pool config — the SSOT for the tokio (AP / Linux)
  host's session-link RECEIVE slot-pool dimensions.

  R2739. This is a MIGRATION AND A CORRECTION, not a new pool. The AP node
  already declared one, inline, at `deploy/ap_mcu_pair.yaml`
  `ap_node.buffer_pools.session_rx_pool` — with no scxml SSOT and no emit, so
  nothing could consume it and nothing graded its values. That file's own note
  above the MCU block says why the MCU pools moved out: dims "live ONLY in their
  scxml SSOTs ... values NOT duplicated". The AP one was the same migration,
  still pending. The deploy entry is now `{ ref: session_rx_pool_ap }`.

  ## slot-size 65600 is DERIVED, and the old 4096 could not hold one batch

  The inline declaration said `slot_size: 4096` while the SAME node declares
  `qos.batch_size: 65536` — a slot sixteen times too small for one negotiated
  batch. That is the defect `reassembly_pool_ap.scxml` records in its own
  comment, in the other direction: a pool dimension taken from something that is
  not what the link delivers. There it was the 1472-byte AP link mtu, which left
  the TCP reassembly window one byte wide and made `transport-fragmentation`
  unobservable end to end.

  So this value is read off the RECEIVE PATH rather than off a deploy number.
  `wz-runtime-tokio`'s stream reader decodes the length prefix, refuses
  `payload_len > u16::MAX`, and then sizes its frame buffer `w + payload_len`
  where `w` is the prefix width — 2 on the universal path, 4 under
  `transport-lowlatency`. The largest frame a peer can therefore present is
  4 + 65535 = 65539 bytes, prefix included, because the reader keeps the prefix
  in the frame so the codec sees the wire bytes verbatim.

  65536 would be THREE BYTES SHORT on the lowlatency arm — the same one-byte
  window in a different disguise — so the figure is rounded up to the next
  multiple of this node's 64-byte alignment: 65600.

  ## slot-count 64 is the declared value and stays

  Carried from the inline block unchanged, and that is a judgement rather than
  an omission: a slot count here is NOT a correctness bound. `RxSlots::reserve`
  returns `None` when every slot is out, and its contract calls that
  back-pressure rather than an error — "the caller drops the datagram and counts
  it, which is what a bounded receive path must do". So the count trades
  throughput against residency, it does not decide whether a frame can be
  received, and the declared figure needs no re-derivation to be correct.

  At 64 x 65600 the arena is ~4.2 MiB resident. Unlike
  `reassembly_pool_ap.scxml` — whose raise "adds no static footprint" because
  the AP host never instantiates that emit's pool struct — this one IS
  instantiated: it is the link tier's receive slot table, which is the whole
  reason it exists.

  ## section / alignment / cache-policy

  `heap` / 64 / `none`, matching `reassembly_pool_ap.scxml` and the inline AP
  declaration. An AP host has no `sram1` and no DMA cache maintenance to
  perform; the MCU pools' `sram1` / 32 / `maintain` shape does not transfer.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="session_rx_pool_ap" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>64</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>65600</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>heap</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>64</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>none</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
